From bbcc15c84055333bff1f6a1fda3b4c36dd2738d2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Corey <coreysho@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 02:55:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] An inv update sends what is in the inv, not the whole grid

THE CEILING THE BANK WAS UNDER. UpdateInvFullEncoder put every slot of the
component on the wire whether or not anything was in it, and one server
message is encoded into a single fixed ClientSocket.out buffer whose writes go
through DataView - which THROWS past the end rather than wrapping. At up to 7
bytes a slot (the id, the 255 marker, a 4-byte count) a 5000-byte buffer held
713 slots. So how big the bank could be was not a content decision at all: it
was decided by a buffer in the network layer, and the cap was invisible until
a full bank crashed the writer.

Two changes, and the first was the encoder's own todo:

  - the transmitted size is now one past the last OCCUPIED slot. This is safe
    because the client's UPDATE_INV_FULL handler already zeroes every slot
    past the size it was given, so a short transmit clears the tail instead of
    leaving stale icons behind. A placeholder is an Item with count 0, not a
    null, so it still counts as occupied and its id still reaches the client.
    An empty 1410-slot bank went from 4234 bytes to 4; a 200-item one costs
    604.

  - ClientSocket.out is alloc(2) = 30000 bytes, for the case that is genuinely
    full: 1410 slots of stacks over 255 is 9877 bytes. The client's inbound
    buffer was raised to match in the companion commit there - one end alone
    would have been worse than neither.

test() counted three header bytes where encode() writes four. Nothing calls
test() on this encoder today, but a one-byte-short answer is what bites
whoever wires it up later.

AND A SEPARATE BUG FOUND ON THE WAY. INV_MOVEITEM_UNCERT dropped the return of
invAdd, so anything the destination could not fit simply stopped existing -
invDel had already taken it out of the source. INV_MOVEITEM and
INV_MOVEITEM_CERT both drop their overflow at the player's feet; this now does
the same rather than inventing a third answer. The bank's own deposit computes
its overflow beforehand and so never hit it, but the leprechaun's store is a
new caller and silent destruction is not a thing to leave armed.

tools/invfullcheck.ts runs the real encoder against the real packed configs:
the grid has a cell for every bank slot plus the tab padding, an empty bank
transmits nothing, a gap in the middle is still transmitted, a placeholder
counts as occupied, the worst case fits, and test() predicts what encode()
writes. Reverting either change makes it go red.

